```

# 6. COMMANDS AND WHAT THEY DO

| Command | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| mkdir | Creates a directory. | mkdir data |
| cd | Moves into a directory. | cd Library_Inventory |
| touch | Creates an empty file. | touch books.txt |
| echo | Adds text with redirection. | echo "Book details" >> books.txt |
| ls | Lists files and folders. | ls |
| grep | Searches matching text. | grep ',Available$' books.txt |
| cut | Extracts selected fields. | cut -d ',' -f2 books.txt |
| sort | Sorts lines in a file. | sort books.txt |
| head | Displays first lines. | head -n 3 books.txt |
| tail | Displays last lines. | tail -n 3 books.txt |
| less | Opens a file for scrolling. | less books.txt |
| cat | Displays or combines files. | cat available.txt |
| mv | Moves or renames a file. | mv old.txt new.txt |
| chmod | Changes file permissions. | chmod 640 final_inventory.txt |
| egrep | Searches using extended patterns. | egrep 'Fiction\|Technology' books.txt |
| fgrep | Searches fixed text. | fgrep 'Atomic Habits' books.txt |

# 7. IMPLEMENTATION: COMMANDS, STEPS AND OUTPUT

## Step 1 – Create Project Folders and Files

Open Ubuntu Terminal and run the following commands:

```bash
mkdir Library_Inventory
cd Library_Inventory
mkdir data reports
cd data
touch books.txt available.txt issued.txt
cd ..
```

These commands create the project folder, data and reports folders, and the empty text files.

## Step 2 – Enter Book Records

Move into the data folder and add the sample entries. The >> operator appends each record to books.txt.

```bash
cd data

echo "B101,The Alchemist,Paulo Coelho,Fiction,Available" >> books.txt
echo "B102,Atomic Habits,James Clear,Self-help,Issued" >> books.txt
echo "B103,Computer Networks,Tanenbaum,Technology,Available" >> books.txt
echo "B104,Rich Dad Poor Dad,Robert Kiyosaki,Finance,Available" >> books.txt
echo "B105,The Hobbit,J.R.R. Tolkien,Fiction,Issued" >> books.txt
echo "B106,Python Basics,Reema Thareja,Technology,Available" >> books.txt
echo "B107,Ikigai,Hector Garcia,Self-help,Available" >> books.txt
echo "B108,DBMS Concepts,Korth,Technology,Issued" >> books.txt
echo "B109,The Diary of a Young Girl,Anne Frank,Biography,Available" >> books.txt
echo "B110,Wings of Fire,A.P.J. Abdul Kalam,Biography,Issued" >> books.txt

cat books.txt
```

The cat command displays the catalogue so that all entries can be checked.

## Step 3 – Separate Available and Issued Books

Run the following commands:

```bash
grep ',Available$' data/books.txt > data/available.txt

grep ',Issued$' data/books.txt > data/issued.txt

cat data/available.txt

cat data/issued.txt
```

The grep command checks the final status field. The > symbol saves matching lines in separate files. This makes it easy to view books according to their availability.

## Step 4 – Search and Extract Book Information

Search for a particular title:

```bash
grep -i "alchemist" data/books.txt
```

Search for books in the Technology category:

```bash
grep ",Technology," data/books.txt
```

Extract book titles:

```bash
cut -d ',' -f2 data/books.txt
```

Search Fiction or Technology books:

```bash
egrep ",(Fiction|Technology)," data/books.txt
```

Search for exact fixed text:

```bash
fgrep "Atomic Habits" data/books.txt
```

The first command searches for a title without depending on letter case. The second finds Technology books. The cut command extracts the second field (Title). The egrep command matches either category, and fgrep searches for exact fixed text.

## Step 5 – Sort and View the Catalogue

Sort books alphabetically by title:

```bash
sort -t ',' -k2,2 data/books.txt > reports/sorted_books.txt
```

Display the first three records:

```bash
head -n 3 reports/sorted_books.txt
```

Display the last three records:

```bash
tail -n 3 reports/sorted_books.txt
```

View the complete sorted catalogue:

```bash
less reports/sorted_books.txt
```

The sort command uses the comma as the field separator and sorts by the second field (Title). The head command displays the first three lines, tail displays the last three, and less allows the user to scroll through the file.

Press q to exit less.

## Step 6 – Prepare the Final Inventory Report

Combine available and issued books:

```bash
cat data/available.txt data/issued.txt > reports/final_inventory.txt
```

Display the first five records:

```bash
head -n 5 reports/final_inventory.txt
```

Display the complete inventory report:

```bash
cat reports/final_inventory.txt
```

The cat command combines the available and issued lists in the given order and writes them into final_inventory.txt.

The > symbol creates or overwrites the destination file, whereas >> appends content to an existing file.

