# 9. HOW THIS SIMPLIFIES A REAL-WORLD ISSUE

A librarian in a small library may need to check whether a title is available, prepare a list of books in a category, or make a separate list of issued books.

Checking every entry manually takes time and can cause errors.

In this project:

- grep searches titles, categories, and book status.
- cut extracts book titles.
- sort arranges the catalogue alphabetically.
- head and tail display selected records.
- cat displays and combines files to create reports.

These operations make basic catalogue handling quicker, more organised, and easier to verify.

# 10. LIMITATIONS AND POSSIBLE IMPROVEMENTS

**Limitations:**

- This is a learning project, not a complete library management application.
- The files do not automatically validate entries or update a lending transaction.
- Plain text files are not suitable for secure multi-user management of a large library.
- The project requires manual commands to perform operations.

**Possible Improvements:**

- Add more books and categories.
- Add more search options.
- Update book status when a book is issued or returned.
- Generate updated inventory reports.
- Use a database for managing a larger library.

# 11. CONCLUSION

The Library Book Inventory and Search System demonstrates how Linux commands can solve simple file-management and text-search tasks without writing a program.

Using commands such as mkdir, touch, echo, grep, cut, sort, head, tail, less, and cat, a small library catalogue can be organised, searched, filtered, and reported in a practical way.

This project also provides basic knowledge of Ubuntu/Linux terminal operations, file handling, text processing, and directory management.

